WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.251 --> 00:00:05.171
I started
with Starbucks back in August of 2010.

00:00:05.296 --> 00:00:08.883
I am in the arts
in the United States of America.

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:11.469
And so I needed some way to get health
benefits.

00:00:11.553 --> 00:00:14.764
And Starbucks was a company
that had touted that

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:16.474
they were very progressive.

00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:20.895
And one of the things that they offered
was benefits for part time employees.

00:00:21.312 --> 00:00:25.150
It was really the company it professed
to be at the beginning of all of this.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.903
And unfortunately, in the last few years,
we've started to see that that slide.

00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:31.865
And so it was time to make some changes.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.493
Across the U.S.,
The pandemic has spurred service workers

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:39.164
to protest workplace conditions
and fight for better treatment and wages.

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:42.250
Some,
like Michelle, have tried to form a union.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.628
In the United States,
that can be a daunting task.

00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:49.424
According to the Bureau of Labor
Statistics, in 2021

00:00:49.591 --> 00:00:54.054
15.8 million wage and salary workers
were represented by a union

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:59.100
with only 1.6% of members belonging
to the food and drinking service industry.

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:00.727
I had made the decision

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:04.647
after having worked through this pandemic
for a year and a half that I was done.

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:05.565
I was tired.

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:07.901
I was overwhelmed and overworked.

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:11.905
And I was watching my coworkers
struggle to pay their rent

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.949
and put groceries in their fridge
while working a full time job.

00:01:14.949 --> 00:01:19.412
I'm certainly fighting this for myself,
but I'm also fighting it for my coworkers

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:23.083
and for future workers
in the service industry.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.090
This is Michelle Eisen.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.219
She's a theater artist, stage manager,
and organizing member of Starbucks

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:35.220
Workers United.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.307
After working for Starbucks locations
in the Elmwood neighborhood of Buffalo,

00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:43.394
and on the island of Oahu in Hawaii
as a barista for 11 years

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:47.482
and experiencing the perils of COVID 19,
Michelle believes she was seeing

00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:49.526
a troubling shift
in the company's culture.

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:52.987
The catalyst for where we are
now certainly was the pandemic.

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:56.449
We were called on to be essential workers
in the middle of this pandemic

00:01:56.449 --> 00:02:00.328
and told us that it was essential
for us to be there serving

00:02:00.328 --> 00:02:01.913
coffee to our communities.

00:02:01.913 --> 00:02:05.333
And on top of that,
we were told that our company

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:08.503
was making record breaking profits
in the middle of this pandemic.

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:09.754
You know, we have a CEO

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:13.508
who's bragging about the level of profits
that have been raked in by this company.

00:02:13.508 --> 00:02:16.177
And none of that is being passed
down to the workers.

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:17.595
And so that was the moment

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:21.850
when we really sort of started
to evaluate the company we worked for

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.979
and who they profess to be
and realize that something had to change.

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:31.234
In August of 2021, a coworker approached
Michelle, inviting her for a cup of coffee

00:02:31.401 --> 00:02:34.028
and presented her with the idea of forming
the union.

00:02:34.362 --> 00:02:38.741
She went point by point and laid out
sort of what had been happening around me

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:41.744
is that several baristas
from several different stores in

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:46.291
Buffalo had been talking and they were
is exhausted as I was and as overwhelmed

00:02:46.291 --> 00:02:50.712
and underappreciated and had decided
that things needed to change.

00:02:50.712 --> 00:02:54.215
And this looks like an option
and I said, "Okay,

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:59.637
I'm on board." "What do you need from me?"
In August of 2021, Michelle got to work.

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:02.724
She and other members of Starbucks
Workers United

00:03:02.724 --> 00:03:06.352
prepared to file petitions
with the National Labor Relations Board

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:09.689
to hold union elections
for three Starbucks locations in Buffalo.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:15.111
Once the union is recognized by the NLRB,
a union contract or collective

00:03:15.111 --> 00:03:18.656
bargaining agreement is negotiated
between workers and management.

00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:21.075
The contract sets forth the pay,

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:24.495
benefits, policies
and working conditions at the company.

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:29.584
We filed the first three petitions
August 30th, and from that point on

00:03:29.584 --> 00:03:34.964
it has been nonstop corporate presence
and interference in this campaign.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:38.384
Starbucks,
however, says there's a misconception

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.803
around the idea
that they're fighting unionization.

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:45.516
Reggie Bores, the director of corporate
communications and inclusivity

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:48.937
and Diversity Communications
said: "We just don't believe

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:53.274
a union is necessary at Starbucks."
Once the union petitions were filed,

00:03:53.524 --> 00:03:56.653
Starbucks began sending executives
to the Buffalo locations.

00:03:56.653 --> 00:04:00.782
Bores said that company leaders
visit stores all the time to help

00:04:00.782 --> 00:04:04.452
and support employees
not to spy on or intimidate them.

00:04:04.953 --> 00:04:07.205
They started showing up at these stores,
particularly

00:04:07.205 --> 00:04:10.667
the ones that had filed petitions
saying they were only there to help.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.462
They were there totally coincidentally,
and it just happened to fall, you know,

00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:16.422
three days after these petitions filed.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:20.051
They also assigned
what they called support managers.

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:21.844
So they flew in other store managers

00:04:21.844 --> 00:04:25.807
from stores across the country, and
they assigned them to all of these stores

00:04:25.807 --> 00:04:26.432
in Buffalo.

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.393
What they support
managers were actually assigned

00:04:28.393 --> 00:04:32.605
to was to surveil
the workers at every possible moment.

00:04:33.231 --> 00:04:36.734
We've heard from store managers
that are no longer with the company,

00:04:36.734 --> 00:04:41.155
that they were told that workers
were never to be left unattended.

00:04:41.197 --> 00:04:44.325
There was always supposed to be either
a store manager or a support manager

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:49.163
on the floor to break up any conversation
that might arise about the union.

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:53.626
It was scary for my coworkers to feel like
their every move was being watched.

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:54.252
Nobody wants to

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:58.131
come into their workplace every day
and feel like they're being watched.

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:01.426
Despite a corporate executive presence

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:05.179
Starbucks Workers United pressed forward
to a historic victory.

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.140
Winning the first union Starbucks

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:11.269
in the country, which we did on December
9th, was amazing.

00:05:11.269 --> 00:05:13.271
To be able
to stand there with my coworkers

00:05:13.271 --> 00:05:15.481
and be the first one to do that.

00:05:15.523 --> 00:05:16.983
That was fantastic.

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:19.610
The movement of unionization for Starbucks
locations

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:22.238
has grown
since the Elmwood December 9th win.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:28.036
I believe we were in 27 states
and had 103 petitions filed.

00:05:28.036 --> 00:05:31.873
Today, we were here in Buffalo
for what was going to be

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:34.709
the next vote count
for three other additional stores

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:37.670
that had filed union
petitions back in November.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:41.758
But that vote count for those
additional stores never happened due to

00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:46.095
what union advocates said was a deliberate
anti-union strategy by Starbucks.

00:05:46.387 --> 00:05:49.390
An announcement from the NLRB stated
that the votes

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:53.102
would be impounded because of a request
for review filed by Starbucks.

00:05:53.102 --> 00:05:57.607
Under the Trump administration,
the NLRB allowed either party, company

00:05:57.607 --> 00:06:00.276
or employee to request review of petitions

00:06:00.276 --> 00:06:04.030
if the request is filed
within ten business days of the decision.

00:06:04.238 --> 00:06:06.407
Starbucks
made their request on the 10th day.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:10.870
Ian Hayes, the workers' attorney, spoke
at a press conference about this decision.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.538
"Starbucks knows what it's doing.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:14.749
Starbucks' attorneys
know what they're doing.

00:06:15.458 --> 00:06:18.795
They were gaming the system
to try to delay the process,

00:06:19.045 --> 00:06:23.383
break the workers momentum just like
they have been for the last half year.

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:29.055
And this right here is what union busting
looks like in the United States in 2022."

00:06:30.014 --> 00:06:30.431
Bores stated

00:06:30.431 --> 00:06:33.893
that Starbucks is simply adhering
to the NLRB's due process,

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:37.855
nothing is being done deliberately
to delay or slow down the vote count.

00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:40.983
So these workers yet again who had worked

00:06:40.983 --> 00:06:44.112
so hard to get to
this point, had their voices silenced.

00:06:44.278 --> 00:06:46.989
It's unfortunately what had happened
in Mesa.

00:06:46.989 --> 00:06:47.698
Last week.

00:06:47.698 --> 00:06:51.828
I flew out to Mesa to support that store,
and we had to go through the exact

00:06:51.828 --> 00:06:53.955
same thing right before their vote count.

00:06:54.038 --> 00:06:57.750
It is another delay tactic
after delay tactic after delay tactic

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.585
that the company has employed.

00:06:59.585 --> 00:07:02.713
Public support from members of government
have pushed the workers

00:07:02.713 --> 00:07:04.424
to continue their fight.

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:08.678
I think we're looking at a new frontier
in the labor movement in this country.

00:07:09.011 --> 00:07:12.515
During the pandemic,
a moment called "Striketober" sparked

00:07:12.515 --> 00:07:13.933
an influx of protests.

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:17.061
From Deere and Companies'
factories, Kellogg's U.S.

00:07:17.061 --> 00:07:21.107
plants, to nurses in Massachusetts,
and distillery workers in Kentucky.

00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:25.194
Tens of thousands of union workers
across a variety of industries

00:07:25.194 --> 00:07:27.405
were either on strike or close to it.

00:07:27.697 --> 00:07:30.199
However, despite ongoing activism,

00:07:30.408 --> 00:07:33.369
the rate of union membership
is down from previous years.

00:07:33.744 --> 00:07:37.373
The Bureau of Labor Statistics notes
that the rate of union membership

00:07:37.373 --> 00:07:42.837
was 10.3% in 2021 and is equivalent
to pre-pandemic levels of 2019.

00:07:43.504 --> 00:07:46.674
I think that our laws are a little bit
behind, unfortunately,

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:51.345
and we're going to have to reform them
and adjust them if we want to see

00:07:51.345 --> 00:07:53.306
this labor movement really take off.

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:56.100
And I think that the NLRB is on board
with that.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:00.021
I think that they also want to be
the agency that protects the worker.

00:08:00.188 --> 00:08:04.192
So we're just waiting for everything
to sort of catch up with our movement.

00:08:06.068 --> 00:08:06.736
I think what's

00:08:06.736 --> 00:08:11.115
kept me fighting this fight is knowing
that I'm on the right side of history.

